In beta plus decay, a proton converts to a neutron, emitting a positron (β+) and an electron neutrino (νe). The mass of a proton is approximately 1.007276 amu, and the mass of a neutron is approximately 1.008665 amu. The mass of an electron is approximately 0.00054858 amu.

Let $M_{13N}$ be the mass of $^{13}$N. The mass of the daughter nucleus, $^{13}$C, is approximately 12.99876 amu. The mass of the positron (β+) is approximately 0.00054858 amu. The mass of the electron neutrino is essentially zero.

The energy released in beta plus decay is equal to the mass difference between the parent and daughter nuclei plus the kinetic energy of the positron.
$Q = (M_{13N} - M_{13C}) + K_{max}$
$Q = (M_{13N} - 12.99876) + 1.19 \text{ MeV}$

The mass difference is the mass of the proton minus the mass of the neutron plus the mass of the electron:
$M_{13N} - M_{13C} - m_p + m_n - m_e = 1.007276 - 1.008665 + 0.00054858 = -0.00084042$ amu

Converting the kinetic energy to energy in amu:
$1.19 \text{ MeV} = 1.19 \times 1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ Joules}$
$1 \text{ Joule} = 1.602 \times 10^{-27} \text{ kg} \cdot \text{m}^2/\text{s}^2$
$E = \frac{1.19 \times 1.602 \times 10^{-13}}{1.602 \times 10^{-27}} \approx 1.19 \times 10^{14} \text{ eV}$
$1 \text{ eV} = 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \text{ Joules}$
$E = 1.19 \times 10^{14} \times \frac{1.602 \times 10^{-19}}{1.602 \times 10^{-27}} \approx 1.19 \times 10^{14} \times 10^{8} = 1.19 \times 10^{22} \text{ eV}$
